==== Wave 1: Mechanics

The SR22 is the classroom for the systems every later wave assumes: the glass cockpit, the autopilot, and the planning workflow that turns a flight into a route. None of it is difficult — the difficulty is in the discipline, and this chapter is about the discipline.

===== The Glass Cockpit

The SR22's panel replaces the 172's round gauges with two large screens. The primary flight display (PFD) puts airspeed, attitude, altitude, heading, and vertical speed together in one place, with the autopilot's targets displayed alongside so you can cross-check what you asked the aircraft to do against what it is actually doing. The multi-function display (MFD) is the map: the moving map draws your position on the route, and with a flight plan loaded it shows the waypoints, the legs, and the procedures ahead. Some installations add synthetic vision and terrain, where the screen draws the ground ahead of you with terrain and obstacles highlighted, so the map becomes a window.

The glass changes the instrument scan in a subtle way: all the numbers are in one place, but the scan still matters, because the danger of a big display is fixation. The classic scan still applies — attitude, airspeed, altitude, heading, with the map for the big picture — and the skill is in sweeping across the displays rather than staring at the prettiest one. Learn to read the glass the way you read the round gauges: as a set of instruments you move between, not a movie you watch.

===== Autopilot Basics

The autopilot is a crewmember, not a replacement. The SR22's autopilot holds the modes you select: heading select turns when you tell it to and holds the course; altitude select climbs or descends to the target and holds it; vertical speed manages the rate; and navigation tracking follows the loaded route through the GPS. Learn them in the order of use — heading and altitude first, navigation tracking once the route is in the box — and they become the same ideas you will manage at airline scale in the 737.

The discipline has two rules. First, engage with the aircraft trimmed and stable: the autopilot flies what it is given, and handing it an untrimmed, unstable aircraft is handing it a problem. Second, disengage by hand before you need to — the autopilot should never fight you, and the pilot who reaches for the disconnect at the same moment as the problem is the pilot who is already behind. The autopilot reduces workload; it does not remove it. You still manage power, configure the aircraft, and watch the weather, and the moment you stop doing those because "the autopilot is flying" is the moment you are no longer the pilot.

===== Flight Planning Workflow

Wave 1 is where flying becomes navigation, and navigation is a workflow that starts before the engine. Build the route in LittleNavMap — departure, destination, route altitudes, fuel stops, alternates — and check the weather along the way, deciding that the flight is legal and sane: VFR minimums at both ends, fuel with margin, and an alternate you could actually reach. Export the plan in the sim's format and load it into the aircraft's avionics, so the map and the autopilot see the route you planned. Then fly it: the moving map and the autopilot fly the plan while you manage the aircraft and the decisions. And review it after landing, because LittleNavMap keeps the track and the debrief — where you drifted, what you would change — is where the learning lives.

The workflow is the same skeleton every later wave uses, with more elaborate tools bolted on. Learn it now, simply, and the FMS of Waves 2-5 is just this workflow with a bigger screen.

===== Navigation & Procedures

The SR22's avionics include the classic navaids alongside the GPS, and the fundamentals matter even in the age of the moving map. VOR navigation is a needle and a course: tune the station, set the course, and fly the needle, and the skill transfers directly to the ILS, where the localizer and glideslope needles are the same idea guiding you to the runway. The approach flow is worth learning before you need it: brief the plate in AviTab, fly to the initial fix, join the final course, and decide early on a go-around if it does not feel right — the decision that feels late in the pattern is the decision that is late in the weather.

Radio work grows up in this wave. Read back clearances, announce positions at non-towered fields, and let the sim's ATC (or a busy frequency) make you practice out loud: the phraseology is a language, and the only way to be fluent is to speak it.

===== Weather Decision-Making

The professional's relationship with weather is numbers, not feelings. A METAR and a TAF tell you the wind, the visibility, the ceiling, and the trend — the numbers that make or break a VFR flight — and reading them before every leg is the habit that keeps a ferry pilot employed. The destination is only the destination if you can also leave it: fuel and alternates are part of the plan, not an afterthought, and the day's route should always know where Plan B is.

The go/no-go call is the real curriculum. It is easier to explain a cancelled flight than a forced landing, and the professional's rule is that the call gets made with a clear head on the ground, never with the fuel low and the ceiling dropping. Wave 1's real test is not the flying; it is making the call, calmly, and being right.
